::kind(api)

::meta{crate: tomet-semantics, dependencies: list(tomet-address, tomet-ast, tomet-ast-parser, tomet-declaration, tomet-semantics-core, tomet-span, tomet-tree, tomet-type), dependents: list(tomet, tomet-ast-parser, tomet-cli, tomet-config, tomet-deps, tomet-html, tomet-indexer, tomet-links, tomet-load, tomet-lsp, tomet-markdown, tomet-pandoc, tomet-printer, tomet-search, tomet-stats, tomet-style, tomet-tests, tomet-transform, tomet-typst, tomet-validator, tomet-validator-blueprint, tomet-validator-core, tomet-validator-profile, tomet-validator-rule, tomet-vault, tomet-workspace)}

=[tomet-semantics]

==[tomet-semantics]

I/O-free semantic classification and normalization layer for Tomet AST elements.

==[Architecture & Responsibilities]

- ("Pure I/O-Free Semantic Layer") `tomet-semantics` is strictly a pure classification and semantic extraction layer.
It performs zero I/O and does not depend on `tomet-parser`, allowing downstream
consumers (`tomet-html`, `tomet-markdown`, `tomet-links`, `tomet-tui`, `tomet-lsp`) to
classify elements without pulling in the parser.
- ("Declarations are not here") What `::kind`, `::meta`, `::macros`, `::vars` and the other standard declarations @em[mean] --
their slots, how often each may be written, what reading one gives -- is
`tomet-declaration`'s, so a `.ctmt` file reads them the same way. This crate keeps
what only a tomet document does with them: [`ElementKind::Declaration`] classifies one,
[`DocumentConfig`] fills the export settings from `::export`, and a vocabulary
binds through `::use`. Which elements carry an address (`@link`, `@embed`, `@file`,
`@dir`) is `tomet-address`'s.

==[Core Capabilities]

- ("Element Classification (`kind`)") Canonical recognition of Tomet's built-in vocabulary
(`::meta` and the other declarations, `@link`, `@embed`, `hr`, `em`, `strong`, `mark`,
`raw`, `quote`, `table`, `heading`, `bare`, `interp`) via [`ElementKind`].
- ("Link Target Extraction (`target`)") Canonical extraction of link targets and scheme
prefix classification (`Url`, `File`, `Tm`, `Id`, `Ref`) via [`TargetScheme`].
- @em[]Positional Argument Normalization (`positional`))

| Unifies positional argument mapping
| (e.g. `@raw(rust)` -> `{lang: "rust"}`) for built-in and vocabulary-declared schemas.

- ("Document Configuration (`config`)") Reads a document's `::export` into a structured [`DocumentConfig`], with the `::macros` and
`::vars` it declares.
- ("Dictionaries (`dictionary`)") Reads a `::dictionary(name)` document into a [`Dictionary`] of [`Term`]s, collects the ones a
vault registers in [`LoadedDictionaries`], and resolves what a `@term` names ([`TermRef`]).
- ("Structural Helpers") Heading level clamping (`heading`) and
table parsing (`table`).
- ("Element Data Extraction (`data`)") Extracts and normalizes an element's `{...}` data group via [`data::element_data`].
- ("Element-Specific Semantics (`elements`)") Dedicated normalization and extraction
logic for concrete built-in element families (`config`, `footnote`,
`heading`, `table`, `tag`, `target`).

==[Module data]

An element's value as data.

`{...}` is the only data syntax cotomet has; `element_data` is the single
place every caller reads an element's `{...}` group through, so none of
them need to know `Group`'s shape themselves.

===[Functions]

====[fn element_data]

An element's value as data: a `{...}` group's `key: value` pairs, or
`None` if the element has no value group at all.

====[fn declaration_data]

[`element_data`] for a declaration.

==[Module dictionary]

Dictionaries of terms: reading a `::dictionary` document, and looking a term up.

A dictionary defines words a vault's documents use, once, so that a document mentions one with
`@term` instead of writing it out. It is a document of its own:

```text
::dictionary(tomet){
  "vault.config.tmt": @term[ The file that marks a vault. ]#(vault-config)
}
```

The key is the term, quoted, so a word in any language fits. The content of the `@term` is its
description. `#(vault-config)` is an optional stable identifier: the term may be renamed and a
mention by identifier does not change. A term and an identifier are each unique within their
dictionary, the way an id is unique within its document.

This is the pure half. Finding and reading the files a vault registers with `::dictionaries` is
`tomet-load`'s, the same split as for vocabularies ([`crate::vocabulary`]).

===[Structs]

====[struct Term]

One term a dictionary defines.

====[struct Dictionary]

A dictionary: a name and its terms, in the order written.

====[struct LoadedDictionaries]

The dictionaries a vault registered, by the name each gives itself.

A dictionary that cannot be accepted is reported in `errors` and left out, not fatal: one bad
file must not hide the state of every document.

===[Enums]

====[enum TermRef]

What a `@term` mention names, read from its one argument.

```text
@term(tomet#vault-config)               by identifier, in a dictionary
@term(tomet#name("vault.config.tmt"))   by name, in a dictionary
@term("vault.config.tmt")               by name, in any dictionary
```

This is the shape `<document>#<place>` with the dictionary as the document, and `name("...")` as a
step like `item(2)`. Nothing on the left of the `#` means this document, as it does everywhere: inside
a dictionary, `#vault-config` is a term of that dictionary.

====[enum TermProblem]

Why a mention names no term.

==[Module elements]

===[elements]

Semantic interpretations and normalization helpers for concrete built-in elements.

While the root of `tomet-semantics` provides the general semantic framework
(element classification, vocabulary schemas, positional argument normalization,
and connect evaluation), this module houses the logic specific to individual
built-in element families:

- (config) Document configuration (`::config`, `::settings`).
- (footnote) Footnote collection and indexing (`@ftn(id)[...]`, and `@ftn(id)` to refer to one).
- (heading) Heading level calculation and bounds clamping (`heading`).
- (table) Table rows and cell structures (`@table`).
- (tag) Tag collection (`#tag`).
- (target) Target URI and scheme resolution (`@link`, `@embed`, `@file`, `@dir`).

===[Module config]

====[Structs]

=====[struct DocumentConfig]

What a document declares about itself that is not formatting or a registry: where it is exported
(`::export`), and the macros and variables it defines (`::macros`, `::vars`).

====[Enums]

=====[enum ExportType]

Supported target format for document exports.

====[Functions]

=====[fn document_config]

Returns this document's `::export`, `::macros` and `::vars`.

`::export` is read in either form, `(type: commonmark, path: "README.md")` or the same keys in
`{...}`; a `path` is one path for every type, or a map of format to path.

===[Module footnote]

Footnote collection, sequential numbering, and cross-reference resolution.

====[Structs]

=====[struct FootnoteItem]

An individual footnote entry resolved within a document.

=====[struct FootnoteRegistry]

Registry storing all resolved footnotes and element back-references for a document.

===[Module heading]

Extracts the `level` of a heading-shaped element (`Heading`, per
[`crate::classify_std`]) from its `args`, centralizing the `1..=6` clamp
that `tomet-html`/`tomet-markdown`/`tomet-printer` each used to apply separately.

====[Functions]

=====[fn heading_level]

The clamped `1..=6` heading level of a heading-shaped element, if it
has one. `None` for any non-heading element, or for a heading whose
`level` argument is missing or not an integer (e.g. hand-authored
`@heading(level: "two")`) -- callers fall back to a default level
themselves, this function never guesses one.

===[Module table]

====[Structs]

=====[struct TableCell]

One cell in a `@table` row, holding trimmed inline nodes.

=====[struct TableRow]

One row in a `@table`, holding cells.

====[Functions]

=====[fn parse_table_rows]

Parses table rows and cells from `@table`'s `[content]` (`Vec<Block>`
now). The row/cell syntax (`[cell][cell]`) is literal text scanned
character-by-character below, not real nested elements, so this just
flattens every `Paragraph`'s own inline content into one sequence first
-- there is realistically only ever one (no blank line inside a
table's rows), but this doesn't assume that.

===[Module tag]

Extracts tags from an element (`@tag(...)`, `@tag[...]`).

====[Functions]

=====[fn extract_tags]

Extracts a list of tag names from a tag element.
Inspects `args` (as string, seq, or map) and `content` (as plain text).

===[Module target]

Extracts the raw target string of a link-shaped element (`Link`/`Embed`,
per [`crate::classify_std`]) under their one canonical `target` key, and
classifies @em[what kind] of target that string is (`TargetScheme`) by its
own scheme prefix -- `url`/`file`/`tm`/`id`/`ref` are no longer carried
by which argument key was used (there's only ever `target:` now); they
live entirely in the target string itself. This is the canonical
extraction, adopted by `tomet-html`/`tomet-markdown`/`tomet-links`
instead of each reading `el.args` raw.

====[Functions]

=====[fn link_target]

The raw target string of a link-shaped element, if it has one. `kind`
should be `classify_std_lenient(el)`'s result -- only `Link`/`Embed` ever return
`Some`, everything else returns `None`.

`tm:path/to/doc#some-id`'s `#fragment` is returned verbatim, still
attached -- splitting it off is a caller concern, not done here.

Deliberately stricter than the existing renderers: a non-string value
under `target` (e.g. `target: 42`) returns `None` rather than a
stringified `"42"`, which would be a wrong filesystem-lookup target
even though it's harmless for display purposes.

=====[fn path_target]

The path a `@file`/`@dir` names, or `None` for anything else.

No scheme prefix to strip: the element name already said which of the
two this is, so a `file:`/`dir:` inside the value would be saying it
twice. That is the difference from [`link_target`], where one element
serves every scheme and the string has to carry which.

=====[fn link_target_of]

Classifies `el` and extracts its link target in one step, for callers
that don't already have `classify_std_lenient(el)`'s result on hand.

=====[fn path_target_of]

[`path_target`] with the kind it classified to, for a caller sweeping
a document without one in hand.

=====[fn target_scheme]

Classifies a `target` string's scheme and strips a recognized explicit prefix.

==[Module flatten]

Projecting an element's data onto a flat, string-valued attribute map.

Every output format Tomet writes into carries element data as string
pairs and nothing more -- HTML's `data-*`, Pandoc's `Attr`. A Tomet
value is a tree, so something has to give.

The rule is @strong[a readable projection plus an exact copy] -- scalars and
sequences of scalars keep their own flat entry, and whenever that
projection would lose something -- a map anywhere, or a sequence
holding one -- the whole group is additionally serialized as JSON
under [`EXACT_DATA_KEY`]. A reader that finds that key should prefer
it and ignore the projection.

Dotted keys (`m.k` for a nested `k` inside `m`) were considered and
rejected: a Tomet map key may itself contain `.` (the repository's own
`default.config.tmt` has `"url.wiki"`), so `m.k` would be ambiguous
between a nested lookup and a flat key of that name. Element @em[names]
use `.` as a namespace separator, but map keys deliberately do not.

===[Structs]

====[struct FlatData]

An element's data, ready for a string-valued attribute map.

===[Functions]

====[fn is_attribute_key]

Whether `key` can be spliced into an attribute name (`data-{key}`).

Exactly the keys cotomet writes bare (letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.`). A key
that needs quotes -- a path, a key with a space, `"` or `>` -- cannot be
part of an attribute name, and spliced in it would add attributes of its own.
The empty key is the positional marker and is not judged here.

====[fn flatten_element_data]

Flattens `el`'s `(args)` and `{value}` for an attribute map.

The element children a `{...}` group may hold are skipped: they belong
in the output's body rather than its attributes.

====[fn flatten_data]

Flattens the two groups directly.

Callers pass `None` for a group the surrounding mapping has already
consumed -- a heading's level, a link's target -- so it is not emitted
a second time as an attribute.

====[fn scalar_string]

The string a value projects to, or `None` when it cannot project.

A sequence joins with `", "`, which is what the HTML writer has always
done. That is not reversible -- `[p, q]` and the literal `"p, q"`
project identically -- but it stays readable, and the exact copy is
what a reader uses when it needs the difference.

====[fn value_to_json]

Converts a Tomet value to JSON, preserving structure exactly.

`tomet-convert-pandoc`'s own `json_to_value` is this function's
inverse, checked against it in `assert_json_round_trip`.

===[Constants]

====[const EXACT_DATA_KEY]

The attribute a consumer should read instead of the flat projection
when it is present.

====[const POSITIONAL_KEY]

The key [`FlatData::into_pairs`] gives a positional group's value.

==[Module vocabulary]

What a `::vocabulary(ns)` document declares, and how a name resolves
against the namespaces a document has in scope.

Extraction is pure: it takes a parsed [`Document`] and reads it. The
file that document came from is somebody else's problem --
`tomet-load` finds `.tomet/vocabularies/<ns>.vocabulary.tmt`
and reads it, because this layer may not do I/O.

See `docs/spec/vocabulary.tmt` for the normative description. Three
rules from it are enforced here:

- Exactly two namespaces may be written bare -- `std`, because the

language carries it, and the document's own `::kind`, because it is
declared on the first line. Anything brought in with `::use` is
always written out.

- `std` wins every collision, and cannot be shadowed: a vocabulary

that declares a name already in [`builtin_kinds`] is rejected at the
declaration rather than at each use site, which is one error instead
of many.

- `doc` (`RESERVED_NAMESPACES`) is a second namespace tomet carries

itself, resolved the same unconditional way `std` is in
[`Bindings::classify`]/[`Bindings::declaration`] -- but, unlike
`std`, never written bare: `@doc.icon(...)` needs its full name, just
never an `::use(doc)` in front of it.

===[Structs]

====[struct ParamDecl]

One parameter, as an entry of an element's `args:` map declares it.

`param` and not `arg`: parameter is the declaration side, argument is
the call side, so `(args)` needs no rename.

`type:` is a [`Type`] (`tomet-type`), and an argument written for the
parameter is held to it (`tomet check`). A `type:` that names no type is
kept as the reason, not dropped: the parameter is then not checked, and
[`Vocabulary::param_problems`] says so once, where the vocabulary is read.

====[struct ElementDecl]

One element, as a vocabulary declares it.

`args:` and `content:` are read; `@data` is not yet. `about:`, the
element's prose, is not read either.

====[struct ParamProblem]

Something wrong with how a parameter is declared, found where the
vocabulary is read rather than at every use.

====[struct Vocabulary]

The elements one namespace declares.

====[struct LoadedVocabularies]

Every vocabulary a vault declares, with whatever went wrong.

The rules for accepting one live in [`LoadedVocabularies::add`], and
they are pure: a caller hands over a parsed document and the label it
wants errors reported under. Where the document came from -- a file
`tomet-load` read, or source text a wasm host was given -- is not
this type's business, so both get the same verdicts.

====[struct Bindings]

The namespaces in scope for one document.

===[Enums]

====[enum Region]

Where an element is allowed to sit in a document.

Distinct from an element's @em[shape], which is whether it stands as a
block or belongs to running text. `::use` has `Region::Preamble` and is
not a singleton -- you bring in several vocabularies -- which is the
proof the two axes are independent.

====[enum ContentAllow]

`content: { allow: }`'s value -- what a vocabulary's own element may hold
in `[content]`/`|content` (`Vec<Block>`, see `tmtroot/docs/spec/
feature/content-shape.tmt`).

===[Functions]

====[fn builtin_doc_vocabularies]

The vocabularies tomet ships hardcoded in Rust rather than as a vault's
`::vocabulary(ns)` document -- the same reason `std`'s elements are a
Rust table (`builtin_kinds`) rather than a `::vocabulary(std)` document:
see `kind.rs`'s doc comment on why that is a limit of the vocabulary
format, not a permanent design.

`doc.index` declares no elements: an index document's body is `std`
(`ul`/`ol`/`@link`) plus `${filter(...)}`/`${by(...)}`, which are neither
elements nor registered functions -- `tomet-transform::index_query`
pattern-matches them directly, with no dependency on `Bindings` at all.
This vocabulary exists purely so `::kind(doc.index)` resolves instead of
being rejected as unknown.

`doc` (bare namespace, not `doc.index`) is the opposite case: it
declares exactly one element, `icon`, and exists so `@doc.icon(...)`
resolves as running text. `std` used to have its own `icon`, recognized
but never drawing anything (`docs/spec/builtin-elements.tmt`'s old
"recognized, does nothing" table) -- worse than not having it, the same
way `::config(style:)` is worse than unimplemented, because a bare
`@icon` looked like the real spelling while doing nothing. `doc.icon`
replaces it under a namespace that says the opposite by construction:
Tomet resolves and draws nothing here, on purpose, forever -- an
external renderer implements this notation to make it into a picture.
`params` exists so `check_arguments` still catches a typo'd key; it is
not a step toward Tomet ever reading `name`/`pkg` itself.

===[Constants]

====[const RESERVED_NAMESPACES]

Namespaces no vault vocabulary may claim, because tomet already gives
them a hardcoded meaning -- `std` conceptually, and now `doc`.

`std` is not actually checked here today (nothing ever compared a
vocabulary's own namespace string against `"std"`; only individual
element names were checked, via `shadowed_builtins`). `doc` is the first
namespace this list actually enforces.
